+      "namespace": "probability.functional",
+      "logical_name": "approximate-block-symmetry-tensor-recovery-draft",
+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Approximate block symmetry and a recovery-kernel counterexample",
+      "statement": "Prover-written mathematical claim; independent review pending. Let 1<=p<=q and T in H^(tensor(p+q)) tensor W be symmetric in the first p positions. Let Q average the next q positions, P_q average the first q positions, delta=||T-QT||, and b=binom(q,p). Then ||T||<=b||P_qT||+(b+1)delta. The two original block projections commute, so the sharp two-block bound applies to QT. Without the second block symmetry, a zero-error recovery estimate fails: for p=1,q=2,H=R^2, T=(e0 tensor e1-e1 tensor e0) tensor e0 is nonzero but P_2T=0. The error coefficient b+1 is a valid bound whose optimality is not claimed.",
