+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Degree-two tilt bound is a quadratic-form variance bound",
+      "statement": "Prover-written mathematical claim; independent review pending. For a centered isotropic probability mu on R^n with finite fourth moments, define U_2f=(1/2)E[f(X)(X tensor X-I)]. Then ||U_2||_(L2->HS)=(1/2)sup_{A=A^T,||A||_HS=1}sqrt(Var_mu(<AX,X>)). If local exponential moments exist, U_2 equals the actual normalized tilt Taylor tensor operator T_2. Thus even degree two of a universal tilt bound requires a dimension-free quadratic-form variance estimate. Isotropy alone is insufficient: the non-log-concave law with mass 1/(2M) at each of +/-sqrt(M) and remaining mass at zero has Var(X)=1 but ||U_2||^2=(M-1)/4. Finite fourth moments alone do not define exponential tilts near zero.",
